Verdict: PASS. I wrote it to session_plan/eval_task_1.md.

• The fix: /bg now waits for the shell to exit first, then gives the output readers two seconds to finish. Before, a job like sleep 600 & echo hi stayed "running" until the background sleep died. If a reader is still open after the two seconds, the job's output gets a note saying so. Anything the background process writes later is still added below the note, and that process is never killed. That matches the task.
• Tests: I ran only the /bg tests, not the full suite. All 12 passed in about 3 seconds. The new tests cover:
  • the background-process case (finishes within 4s, exit code 0, output starts with hi, note present);
  • late output landing below the note;
  • an ordinary echo hi; exit 3 job, whose output is exactly as before with no note;
  • 2,000 lines plus stderr written just before exit, all kept;
  • a background process with its output redirected away, which gets no note.
• Leftovers: BG_READER_GRACE, BG_HELD_OPEN_NOTE and the finished_within test helper are all used in the diff. git grep '#1063' in src and docs only finds comments explaining the fix, nothing still calling it open.
• Docs: docs/src/usage/commands.md describes the new behaviour, the note text matches the constant, and it names the /bg kill gap as #1077. The ARCHITECTURE.md entry for commands_bg.rs is updated too.

I didn't check two things the task required, because neither shows up in the diff: the comparison against watch_wait.rs and commands_run.rs that was supposed to go in the commit message, and whether issue #1077 was actually filed on GitHub. I also didn't run the deliberate-break check myself.

